# What clang-tidy looks for, and what it has been told not to.
#
# The set is wide on purpose. The checks that find real bugs in C are
# bugprone-*, cert-* and the static analyser, and none of them cost anything
# except the time to run them. Everything switched off below is switched off for
# a reason written next to it, because a checker configured by attrition ends up
# checking nothing and nobody can tell when that happened.
#
# CI pins the clang-tidy version for the same reason it pins clang-format: the
# checks change between releases and a new one appearing in the middle of an
# unrelated pull request is somebody else's afternoon.

Checks: >
  bugprone-*,
  cert-*,
  clang-analyzer-*,
  misc-*,
  performance-*,
  portability-*,
  readability-*,
  -bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters,
  -cert-err33-c,
  -clang-analyzer-security.insecureAPI.DeprecatedOrUnsafeBufferHandling,
  -misc-include-cleaner,
  -misc-no-recursion,
  -performance-no-int-to-ptr,
  -readability-avoid-nested-conditional-operator,
  -readability-braces-around-statements,
  -readability-function-cognitive-complexity,
  -readability-identifier-length,
  -readability-implicit-bool-conversion,
  -readability-isolate-declaration,
  -readability-magic-numbers,
  -readability-math-missing-parentheses,
  -readability-trailing-comma,
  -readability-use-concise-preprocessor-directives

# Why each of those is off:
#
#   bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters
#     str_eq(Str a, Str b) is not a design flaw, it is a comparison. Go's
#     signatures come across as they are and most of them have two parameters of
#     the same type because the operation has two operands.
#
#   cert-err33-c
#     The fatal error path writes its message with fputs and does not look at
#     the result, on purpose. It is already ending the process and there is
#     nowhere to report a failed report to. Every other ignored return in the
#     tree would be a bug, so this one is a comment in panic.c rather than a
#     check for the whole library.
#
#   clang-analyzer-security.insecureAPI.DeprecatedOrUnsafeBufferHandling
#     It wants memcpy_s, memset_s and memmove_s, which are C11 Annex K. Annex K
#     is optional, glibc has never implemented it and has said it will not, musl
#     does not have it either, and the one widely shipped implementation is
#     Microsoft's and predates the annex. So following this check would mean
#     either writing a memcpy_s of our own, which is memcpy with an argument
#     nobody checks, or not calling memcpy, in a library whose job includes
#     copying bytes. Every call it flags has a length computed from a size the
#     caller owns, and the bounds are the thing the tests and the sanitisers are
#     for.
#
#     This check fires on Linux and not on the macOS SDK, which is how the job
#     stayed red on CI while every local run looked clean. Worth knowing before
#     trusting a green tidy on a Mac.
#
#   misc-include-cleaner
#     It wants every file to include the header that declares each symbol it
#     uses, which for this library means every .c file including stdint.h for
#     uint32_t and burrow/core.h for Str. The headers are layered deliberately,
#     core.h is the one that owns the numeric types, and a file that includes
#     burrow/slice.h has already been given them. Following this check would
#     mean writing the layering out again in every file.
#
#   misc-no-recursion
#     errors_is, errors_as and type_is_comparable recurse, because the things
#     they walk are trees. Go's implementations recurse over the same trees.
#
#   performance-no-int-to-ptr
#     An allocator aligns a pointer by casting it to an integer, rounding it and
#     casting it back. There is no other way to write that in C, and the three
#     places that do it are the allocators.
#
#   readability-braces-around-statements, -isolate-declaration
#     House style, set by .clang-format and by the existing tree.
#
#   readability-function-cognitive-complexity, -magic-numbers
#     This is a port. The function is as complicated as the Go function it came
#     from and the constants are Go's constants. Simplifying either would be a
#     rewrite, and a rewrite is how a port stops matching.
#
#   readability-identifier-length
#     i, a, b, n. Go's own code uses them and so does every C library worth
#     reading.
#
#   readability-implicit-bool-conversion
#     Flags `return n == 0` style code that is already clear, and would have us
#     write `!= 0` on things that are already truth values.
#
#   readability-math-missing-parentheses
#     Wants parentheses around * inside +, which is the precedence every C
#     programmer knows.
#
#   readability-trailing-comma, -use-concise-preprocessor-directives
#     Both arrive after the pinned 20.1.0, which reports them as unknown checks
#     under --verify-config, and both disagree with how the tree is already
#     written. They are switched off ahead of time so that moving the pin is one
#     line in CI and not an afternoon of reformatting. Worth revisiting when the
#     pin moves.
#
CheckOptions:
  # _XOPEN_SOURCE is a reserved identifier and being one is the whole point of
  # it. It is the name POSIX picked for asking a libc to declare ucontext and
  # there is no unreserved spelling of it, so src/runtime/context.c defines it
  # before its first include. Naming the one identifier here keeps the check on
  # for everything else, which is better than a NOLINT that grows a second
  # instance nobody notices.
  #
  # Three lines for one check because cert-dcl37-c and cert-dcl51-cpp are
  # aliases of it and each alias reads its options under its own name. Set only
  # the first and the finding comes back with the other two in the brackets.
  - key: bugprone-reserved-identifier.AllowedIdentifiers
    value: '_XOPEN_SOURCE'
  - key: cert-dcl37-c.AllowedIdentifiers
    value: '_XOPEN_SOURCE'
  - key: cert-dcl51-cpp.AllowedIdentifiers
    value: '_XOPEN_SOURCE'

# Warnings from headers are on, because the headers are where most of this
# library lives.
HeaderFilterRegex: 'include/burrow/.*'
WarningsAsErrors: '*'
FormatStyle: file
